  "query_preview": "..A weak and dry shortwave trough will approach \nnorthern Arizona Tuesday night then brush across the area on \nWednesday. The main impact of this system will be the development \nafter midnight of locally gusty south winds 10-20 mph along and \njust north of terrain features across the area from the Mogollon \nRim and White Mountains northward. During the day Wednesday, as \nthe trough begins to head away from Arizona, west to northwest \nwinds at 5-15 mph gusting to around 25 mph will develop. The \nhigher gusts will be primarily located over the mountain areas \nextending from the White Mountains/Mogollon Rim to the New \nMexico/Utah borders. The trough will be well out of the area by \nThursday with a return to light and variable winds.\n\nFrom Friday into Monday...High pressure will build over the \nGreat Basin while a weak low pressure system spins up over Baja\nCalifornia. Arizona will be located between these two systems. A\nmore moderate air mass with this pattern will result in warmer \ndaytime temperatures. In addition, surface pressure gradients \nwill shift to favor north to northeast breezes at 10-20 mph. From \nthe Mogollon Rim southward, in locations where northeast winds \ntypically funnel, look for gusts around 25 mph. There is just a \nhint of lingering moisture on Friday over the White Mountains and \neastern Mogollon Rim as this pattern develops. A a result, the \ncombination of moisture and daytime heating will result in a \nslight chance of a few instability showers developing. Elsewhere, \nremaining dry.\n\nBeyond Monday...A more unsettled pattern develops with chances for\nshowers returning later next week.",
